{
  "image_name": "3fd6ca1f45067787284bc412869abc7e.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure contrasts a healthy intestinal lining with the disrupted state associated with Crohn’s disease, centered on the role of microbial sensing by epithelial cells. On the left, under homeostasis, bacterial peptidoglycan from the gut microbiota is detected through NOD2 and RIP2, activating NF-κB–dependent gene transcription. This supports a protective program in the epithelium, including increased antimicrobial peptides, mucin, and REGIIIγ, stronger barrier function, and immune surveillance. Signals also promote recruitment of monocytes and an early Th17/IL-22 response that appears to help maintain the mucosal barrier.\n\nOn the right, a perturbation such as antibiotics or infection shifts the system toward disease, especially when NOD2 is mutated. The image shows loss of peptidoglycan sensing, reduced mucin and antimicrobial peptides, and failure of the protective inflammatory program. The microbiota becomes altered, leading to dysbiosis and barrier dysfunction. As the epithelial barrier breaks down, immune cells accumulate in the underlying tissue, including neutrophils and T cells, in what is labeled as compensatory immune activation. Rather than restoring balance, this escalates into chronic inflammation. Overall, the figure illustrates how defective innate sensing can convert normal host–microbiota interactions into persistent intestinal inflammation.",
